  <section|Status><label|status>

  Draft awaiting independent review. This proves the weighted two-index
  budget and its momentum-space Cauchy assembly, conditional only on the
  ordinary one-slot path-product estimate explicitly proved in the
  manuscript. It does not close the nonlinear/annulus inputs to the full
  <verbatim|flux_cap> theorem.

  <section|Source correspondence><label|source-correspondence>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Source pin: <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.

    <item>Path data and row budget: <verbatim|YM.RG.PathData>,
    <verbatim|YM.RG.PathData.Bounds.rows>, and
    <verbatim|YM.RG.PathData.ellTerms> in
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/RG/PathActions.lean>.

    <item>The exact pair atom is <verbatim|YM.Stationary.gramPath>; its
    support length and color-summed coefficient magnitude are
    <verbatim|YM.Stationary.gram_size> and
    <verbatim|YM.Stationary.gram_charge>; its evaluation as the inner product
    of two row terms is <verbatim|YM.Stationary.gram_eval> in
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Shape/ClassicalMatch.lean>.

    <item>The weighted sum is <verbatim|YM.Stationary.gram_budget>; it uses
    <verbatim|budget_prod_finite> and factorizes the two independent row
    sums. Its constant is <verbatim|gramCost G C = \|PlaqAxes\| * C^2 *
    (\|ColorIndex\|/2)>.

    <item>The local path-product bound is <verbatim|Work.F02.slotExt_bound>
    and the two-slot atom version is <verbatim|Work.F02.atomExt_bound> in
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Work/F02/Path.lean>. The Cauchy summation chain is
    <verbatim|YM.Stationary.all_ages>, <verbatim|YM.Stationary.term_vault>,
    and <verbatim|YM.Stationary.gathered> in
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Shape/ClassicalMatch.lean> and
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Shape/LocalOracle.lean>.

    <item>The exact application to the path kernel is
    <verbatim|YM.Stationary.quadratic_write> and
    <verbatim|YM.Stationary.column_price> in
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Shape/ClassicalMatch.lean>.
    <verbatim|quadratic_write> splits the local action into the direct Wilson
    term plus the sum over <verbatim|gramPath>; <verbatim|column_price>
    combines the latter's price with the bare Wilson picture from
    <verbatim|YM.Stationary.Wilson_stream>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Mathematical boundary><label|mathematical-boundary>

  The manuscript expands the row-square algebra, its absolute activity sum,
  the length-two holomorphic bound, strip growth, uniform convergence, and
  finite-torus character summation. The countable Cauchy assembly is proved
  in the stated argument using its explicit M-test majorant. The application
  corollary requires the exact decomposition displayed in the statement; for
  the public path kernel this is <verbatim|quadratic_write>.

  Remaining flux inputs include <verbatim|Films.RankColor.honored>,
  <verbatim|Shape.VertexWeights.setWest>,
  <verbatim|OneLoop.Lattice.tideMark>, <verbatim|Loops.Curvature.bequest>,
  <verbatim|Loops.HessianMatch.quoting> and <verbatim|pole_reduced>, plus the
  annulus/response receipts. This unit does not claim their production.

  <section|Checks actually run><label|checks-actually-run>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>No Lean or TeX build run.

    <item><verbatim|python3 scripts/check_blueprint.py> was run and failed
    because <verbatim|blueprint/units/YM-000026/unit.json> is absent in this
    clone; no shared metadata was created.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Final source checks added in this
  revision><label|final-source-checks-added-in-this-revision>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The slot extension is exactly the complex-linear extension of the
    real skew-coordinate functionals applied to half the difference of the
    forward slot string and its reversed string. See
    <verbatim|YM.Work.F02.colorProject>, <verbatim|slotMatrix>, and
    <verbatim|slotExt_physical> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Work/F02/Path.lean>. The
    slot walk has length at most twice the declared slot length;
    <verbatim|slotExt_bound> obtains the exponential bound on a common
    complex ball before any derivative is taken.

    <item>The manuscript explicitly checks normal convergence of the
    two-index row expansion on that common ball, using the product of the row
    budgets. This justifies holomorphy and termwise coefficient extraction
    rather than inferring it from momentum-strip bounds alone.

    <item>The averaged application follows <verbatim|YM.Stationary.rotPix>,
    <verbatim|reflectFee>, <verbatim|pix_firm>, <verbatim|bleed_cap>, and
    <verbatim|bleed_record> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Shape/LocalOracle.lean>.
    For each entry the transformation is a product of <verbatim|t+1>
    reflected-character fees and the original entry at rotated directions and
    rotated momenta. Each fee has price <verbatim|exp((t+1) eta)>; their
    product is exactly bounded by <verbatim|reflectBill t eta = exp((t+1)^2
    eta)>. The final normalized signed average preserves the bound and its
    record identity. <verbatim|column_price> in
    <verbatim|ClassicalMatch.lean> uses this <verbatim|bleed> step to
    represent <verbatim|inputSeries>.

    <item>These are mathematical correspondences checked by source inspection
    only; no Lean build, TeX build, or blueprint checker pass is claimed in
    this revision.
  </itemize-minus>

